From 6a4c4d14ad171a825f5c4ea7b0845311091b230c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:09:22 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Frame interpolation: copies told by their pose, sprites by
 their place

Two kinds of effect were drawn wrongly on the in-between frames, each in
a way the pairing of instances could not see.

Copies of one mesh that start from one point differ only in how they are
turned and sized: the rays of a light, a burst of shards. When one
expires, each copy after it pairs with its neighbour in drawing order,
and that neighbour stands at the very same position, so the check that
a partner is where the camera carries it passed. The in-between frame
swung every copy towards the next. Dissidia 012's light behind Cosmos
does this every 16 game frames. Instances now compare their poses too:
a drawing-order partner must also be turned and sized like the copy, the
nearest pair counts the difference, and a copy with no partner anything
like it stays unpaired. Off: <prefix>_INTERPOLATION_NO_INSTANCE_POSES.

Small sprites written into a scratch buffer each frame have no lasting
address: the same vertices hold another puff of smoke from one frame to
the next, and blending the two drew a cloud's puffs where neither frame
had them. Quads of one texture, format and primitive whose partner is
not where the camera carries it now pair by where they are, whatever
address they read. A quad that is where it should be keeps its partner:
quads with their places in their vertices share one matrix, and only
their addresses tell them apart. Off:
<prefix>_INTERPOLATION_NO_SPRITE_POOLS.

Verified on Windows with Dissidia 012 (USA disc, clang release build,
60 fps, internal scale 2, story scenes played through a test switch).
Checks with more than 5,000 pixels of the halfway frame far from both
frames: the Cosmos scene 18 of 628 before, 0 after; Zidane's ending 338
of 397 before, 1 after; as many frames were cut before as after. Quick
Battles on World of Darkness and Order's Sanctuary drew correctly, with
World of Darkness rendering in 5.75 ms before and 5.69 ms after; fights
differ from run to run, so they do not compare frame by frame. The
framework's tests pass, seven checks of them new; the ad hoc ones were
not run. Not tested: the Steam Deck, Linux, Android, macOS and other
games.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
